  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Saturday...\n\nRecent burn scars (Elk, Remington, East Side & Robertson Draw)\nremain in a Flash Flood Watch til midnight. Not much happening in\nthese areas as of 18z, but expect shower & t-storm development \nsoon with a risk of heavy rain persisting thru the evening. No \nchanges here.\n\nHigh elevation snow showers are likely as snow levels fall to near\n9kft by early Sunday. A Winter Weather Advisory is in effect for\ntravel impacts over the Beartooth Highway, mainly above Vista\nPoint, from midnight tonight through Sunday evening. Confidence\nhas increased that wrap around moisture as the low lifts north\nwill be sufficient to produce minor snow accumulation, though\ngreater amounts (2-5\") will be on W-NW aspects.\n\nThere remains a marginal risk of severe t-storms east of Billings\nthis afternoon & evening. Morning activity has slowed the\ndestabilizing process, but there is clearing over Powder River and\nCarter behind the initial line of convection that moved through\nthis area earlier. Early afternoon heating may give way to a few\nstrong to severe storms, beginning around 21z if the latest HRRR\nis to be believed, though all his res models have performed poorly\nwith this morning's convection.\n\nSeveral areas of moderate to heavy showers have already occurred,\nmainly over the east, north and west (1.38\" of rain measured at\nBig Timber over the past 6hrs). South central areas including\nBillings and Sheridan have been missing out per some mid level \ndrying and surface temps that are cool enough to keep instability \ncapped, but starting to see development over the Pryors and \nBighorns as this is being written.\n\n18z mesoanalysis shows the upper low still well to our south near\nthe WY/UT/CO triple point. There are backing mid level winds (i.e.\nascent) noted in central WY just north of the upper low, and pwats\nare quite high across the region. All told, expect occasional \nshowers & embedded t-storms with locally heavy rain in all areas \nover the next 12hrs as large scale ascent increases and \nlingers...finally lifting to our north late tomorrow. Precip \namounts should generally range between 0.50\" and 1.50\", but no \ndoubt there will be localized heavier amounts w/ the convection. \n\nAs already mentioned, ascent continues thru much of tomorrow, but\ndownslope winds deepen at the same time, and this will have an \nimpact on precip rates and coverage. Greatest potential for \nt-storms Sunday is in our east along the lifting PV max. There may\nbe a brief opportunity for a strong to severe storm in far \nsoutheast MT in the afternoon, and the HREF does show a few modest\nupdraft helicity tracks. Western areas will see lighter rain \nshowers tomorrow, with breezy westerly winds. Temps Sunday will be\ncool with highs in the 50s west to near 70F east.\n\nPrecip for the most part will end Sunday night as the low lifts\nfurther north. Look for afternoon & evening storms Monday as\nanother shortwave lifts out of southern ID. With more favorable \nshear, substantial diurnal heating and easterly surface winds, \nthere may be a risk of strong to severe storms (though models not\nshowing this yet).\n\nUpper low moves slowly and gradually fills Tuesday and Wednesday,\nkeeping us under a cyclonic flow. Still a lower chance of showers\n& t-storms these days, with best chance of storms in the east, and\na gradual warming trend. Next Pacific wave arrives Thursday. \nOverall, active weather persists Monday through Thursday as temps \nwarm back to the 70s. Higher heights and a shift back to SW flow \naloft should push temps back to the 80s by the end of the week. \nFriday looks mostly dry but the next chance of storms may return \nby Saturday as energy emerges from the next Pacific coast trof.\n\nJKL",
